= i
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a nonrelativistic <electron>,
$$
\lambda=\frac hp=\frac h{\sqrt{2m_eE}},\qquad
E=20(1.6\times10^{-19})=3.2\times10^{-18}\ {\rm J}.
$$
Using the supplied constants gives
$$
\boxed{\lambda\simeq2.75\times10^{-10}\ {\rm m}=0.275\ {\rm nm}.}
$$
This is comparable with atomic lattice spacings. A suitable crystalline surface or sufficiently thin crystal can therefore diffract the <electron> beam; the reciprocal-lattice geometry and scattering conditions determine the actual angles. A grating or slit can also diffract matter waves if its scale and coherence conditions are suitable. The <kinetic energy> is tiny compared with the <electron> rest <energy>, so relativistic corrections are negligible at the stated precision.

= ii
{parent=14}
{scope}

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Only $n=1,2$ have energies below $6\epsilon$. Squaring the corresponding expansion coefficients gives $0.2^2+0.3^2=0.13$.

The printed rounded coefficients have norm
$$
S=0.2^2+0.3^2+0.4^2+0.843^2=1.000649.
$$
Thus the expected rounded answer is **$P(E<6\epsilon)\simeq0.13$**. If the decimal coefficients are treated as literal exact inputs and the state normalized, the exact <probability> for that input is **$0.13/S\simeq0.129916$**. This distinction avoids treating rounding error as a physical departure from <wavefunction normalization>.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Orthonormal <energy eigenstates> contribute incoherently to the expectation of <energy>:
$$
\langle E\rangle=
\frac{\epsilon}{S}\left[1(0.2)^2+4(0.3)^2+9(0.4)^2+16(0.843)^2\right]
=\frac{13.210384}{1.000649}\epsilon.
$$
Therefore **$\langle E\rangle\simeq13.2018\epsilon$** for the normalized literal decimals. Ignoring the small rounding-induced norm error gives $13.2104\epsilon$, conventionally reported at this precision as about **$13.2\epsilon$**. The mean need not equal an individual <energy> <eigenvalue>.

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Each <energy> component acquires its own phase:
$$
\psi(x,t)=\frac1{\sqrt S}\left[
0.2e^{-i\epsilon t/\hbar}\psi_1(x)+
0.3e^{-4i\epsilon t/\hbar}\psi_2(x)+
0.4e^{-9i\epsilon t/\hbar}\psi_3(x)+
0.843e^{-16i\epsilon t/\hbar}\psi_4(x)\right].
$$
The $1/\sqrt S$ factor normalizes the printed rounded input; it can be omitted if the coefficients are regarded as an already normalized rounded representation.

**The <energy> probabilities and $\langle E\rangle$ remain constant** under this time-independent <hamiltonian> because phase factors have unit modulus. <position> probabilities can nevertheless vary: interference between different <energy> components generally changes with their relative phases. A stationary <energy> distribution is not the same thing as a stationary spatial <probability distribution>.

= d
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The result $16\epsilon$ projects the state onto the corresponding nondegenerate <energy eigenstate>. Immediately after an ideal measurement,
$$
\boxed{\psi_{\rm after}(x)=e^{i\chi}\psi_4(x)}
$$
for an unobservable overall phase $\chi$. Subsequent isolated evolution adds $e^{-16i\epsilon t/\hbar}$ but does not mix in other <energy eigenstates>. **A repeat ideal <energy> measurement gives $16\epsilon$ with <probability> one**, provided the <hamiltonian> is unchanged and no intervening disturbance occurs.

